              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Describe the structure of a soap molecule and explain how it forms micelles in water."
            }
          ]
        },
        "topic_names": [
          "Soaps and Detergents"
        ],
        "answer": "A soap molecule consists of a long hydrocarbon chain (hydrophobic tail) and a polar ionic end (hydrophilic head). In water, the hydrophobic tail avoids water and orients itself towards oil or grease, while the hydrophilic head interacts with water. When the concentration of soap increases, the molecules arrange themselves into spherical structures called micelles. In a micelle, the hydrophobic tails point inward, trapping oil or dirt, and the ionic ends face outward, interacting with water. This formation allows the micelle to suspend dirt in water and rinse it away, enabling effective cleaning.",
