        "topic_names": [
          "Soaps and Detergents"
        ],
        "answer": "Soaps clean by forming micelles in water. The hydrophobic tail of the soap molecule attaches to oily dirt, while the hydrophilic ionic end faces water. The micelle structure traps the dirt in its core and suspends it in water, allowing it to be rinsed away. However, in hard water, soap reacts with calcium and magnesium ions to form insoluble scum, reducing its cleaning efficiency. Detergents are preferred in hard water because they are salts of sulphonic acids or ammonium salts that do not form precipitates with these ions. Thus, detergents lather well and clean effectively even in hard water.",
